\textbf{Impact of collapse.}
We examine the impact of the collapse mechanism by analyzing the heuristics produced by CALM both without collapse and under various hyperparameter configurations that influence when collapse is triggered. As shown in Table~\ref{tab:ablation}, incorporating the collapse mechanism generally enhances the heuristic search process. An exception arises in the configuration with the strictest tolerance for not discovering a breakthrough heuristic (i.e., when $\delta_0=0.005$ and $C=15$). A detailed analysis of the evolutionary trajectory under this setting reveals a significantly reduced number of breakthroughs. In one run on the OP problem, no breakthrough heuristic was identified after the 132nd LLM query. These findings suggest that setting a reasonable tolerance for the absence of breakthroughs—balancing patience with the benefits of early stopping—is important for supporting a more effective evolution.

\textbf{Impact of operators.}
We evaluate each operator’s contribution by measuring CALM’s performance with that operator removed (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}). Results show all operators positively impact heuristic quality. Crossover, injection, and replacement are similarly critical—removing any one notably degrades performance in either OBP or OP. Among all, removing simplification causes the largest drop in both tasks, likely because it uniquely reduces redundancy and curbs complexity, counterbalancing other operators that tend to increase heuristic length. Moreover, when crossover is applied without diversity-based selection—using only performance-based sampling—CALM performs worse than with no crossover at all, highlighting the importance of diversity awareness in the most-used operator.
%We assess the contribution of each operator by evaluating the heuristics generated by CALM with that operator removed, as presented in Table~\ref{tab:ablation}. The results indicate that each operator positively contributes to the quality of the discovered heuristics. The roles of crossover, injection, and replacement appear similarly important; omitting any one of them can significantly degrade performance in either OBP or OP. Among all operators, removing the simplification operator leads to the most substantial performance drop in both problems. This may be because all other operators tend to increase the complexity or length of the heuristics, whereas simplification uniquely counteracts this tendency by reducing redundancy and promoting concise representations. Additionally, without diversity-based selection, i.e., when crossover operates on heuristics sampled solely by performance, CALM performs worse than that without the whole crossover operator. This reveals the importance in introducing diversity into the operators that called the most frequent.

\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
This paper introduces CALM, the first framework to marry prompt evolution with on-the-fly LLM adaptation for AHD, freeing it from the constraints of fixed-model approaches. Running entirely on a single 24 GB GPU with a compact foundation model, CALM autonomously uncovers heuristics that outmatch SOTA API-based baselines across various challenging optimization scenarios. Moreover, even without the power of RL, CALM matches or exceeds prior best results using the same LLM API, demonstrating the potency of our verbal-gradient designs. In the future, we expect that scaling CALM’s paradigm to larger models and extended post-training could further push the frontier of automated algorithm discovery.
%This paper proposes the first LLM-based AHD method named CALM that simultaneously optimizes both the prompt generation and the LLM model itself, overcoming the limitations of fixed-model approachs. Applying CALM on a limited-capacity foundational LLM with a 24GB-memory GPU for both fine-tuning and inference, heuristics that outperform SOTA LLM-based AHD baselines that rely on a powerful API-based model were discovered at various optimization tasks. Solely employing the proposed prompt generation techniques, API-based CALM still show top-tier or even superior performance in evaluation. Currently, the development of LLM-based AHD is still in a rising stage, and we expect the power of RL combined with the adapted prompt generation principles proposed in this paper could be further unleashed on larger scale of foundational LLMs and post-trainings, pushing the limit of algorithm design.




