In order to infer labels with the complexity framework on \vendor code snippets, code inputs need to be parsable into a dataclass of the format \cref{alg:dataclass_template}. We define the task of dataclass generation as querying a LLM for such dataclass given the problem description and an example solution. Performance is measured by \textsc{\textbf{Corr}}, that accounts for the correction (executability) of the methods \texttt{Input.from\_str} and \texttt{Input.\_\_repr\_\_}, and \textsc{\textbf{BckTr}}, which measures accuracy of the backtranslation: 


~~~~\texttt{\small input\_ == Input.from\_str(input\_).\_\_repr\_\_()}

\textsc{\textbf{Corr@k}} and \textsc{\textbf{BckTr@k}} are unbiased estimators of performance among k samples, following the definition of \cite{chen2021evaluating}.
\cref{tab:dataclass_generation} sums up the benchmark results of the dataclass generation task. \textsc{Llama 3.1 405B} reaches best performance, capable of 58.1\% correct backtranslation for one dataclass out of ten attempts per problem. To further boost performance, several passes corresponding to different solutions per problem are performed, thus generating a correct dataclass for 82\% of \vendor problems. 

\subsection{Complexity Framework Performance}

Using the general dataset and the test splits of \benchmark, the complexity framework is evaluated for its accuracy, coverage and self-consistency.

\textbf{Accuracy}~~A human review measured the accuracy of the labels as output by the complexity framework compared to the labels assigned by a human. On the time complexity test set (after post-processing and filtering), the framework reaches 92\% accuracy on a 50 sample split. On the space complexity test set (after post-processing and filtering), the framework reaches 84\% on a 50 sample split.

\textbf{Coverage}~~\cref{fig:error_analysis} measures the ratio of solutions per problem for which the framework fails to predict a label. Whatever the type of complexity, approximately 84\% of problems have a fail rate  below 30\%, and only 4.5\% of problems have a fail rate above 0.9, for reasons ranging from incorrect generated dataclass to an edge case not covered by the range of tests the framework performs. Most input types are correctly covered by the framework. Exceptions such as tuple and triple-nested lists are infrequent.

\textbf{Self-consistency}~~As it relies on empirical measures of runtimes and memory footprints, the framework is exposed to stochastic noise that can alter the reliability of its complexity predictions. Running the framework 20 times on 10 solutions of every problem and complexity class of the candidate test set, before any filtering based precisely on stability, 91.9\% (resp.~89.1\%) self-consistency is achieved for time (resp.~space) complexity, for a total of 10130 (resp.~10520) different code solutions.

