\subsection{Time-Space Complexity Prediction}
\label{results:first_task}

The first evaluation task of \benchmark, \textbf{Complexity Prediction}, consists in predicting the time and space complexity given a problem description and a human solution. Our baseline for this task is the naive model that always returns $O(n)$, the most frequent class. $Pass@k$ measures the accuracy of finding the correct complexity; $Best@k$ measures accuracy only across the most optimized complexity class of each problem; $All@k$ requires correct complexity output across all complexity classes at once per problem. Results are displayed in \cref{table:complexityeval}. A query example, along with an output example of \textsc{DeepSeek-R1 Llama 70B}, is provided in \cref{appendix:complexity_prediction_example}.

\subsection{Time-Space Complexity Code Generation}
\label{results:second_task}

The second task \textbf{Complexity Generation} requires the LLM to generate a correct solution to a given problem description that has to respect a feasible time or space complexity requirement. Our baseline for this task is a \textsc{Llama 3.1 70B} model that is queried for the same prompts without the complexity requirement. $Pass@k$ measures the accuracy of finding a correct solution, according to public, private and generated tests, that has the correct complexity, as measured by the complexity framework; $Best@k$ and $All@k$ are similarly defined as their counterparts of \cref{results:first_task}. Results are displayed in \cref{table:complexityeval}. An example with \textsc{DeepSeek-R1 Llama 70B} is provided in \cref{appendix:complexity_generation_example}.

