\subsection{Time-Space Complexity Coefficient Percentile Ranking}
\label{results:third_task}

The third task, \textbf{Complexity Coefficient Percentile Ranking}, measures how a generated solution to a given problem, respecting a complexity requirement, ranks among human solutions of the same complexity class and problem. The ranking is performed based on the coefficient of the complexity curve, as measured by the framework: the lower the coefficient, the more flat the complexity curve and the more optimized the solution. Ranking results are given in percentile of the distribution, where a solution of the $nth$ percentile is more optimized than $n\%$ of human solutions. The querying is similar to \cref{results:second_task} with the addition of the requirement "Try to optimize the runtime of your code as much as you can, while respecting the time complexity requirement". Results are displayed in \cref{table:coefficient_ranking}.

\subsection{Complexity Prediction and Generation Fine-tuning}

Using training sets of ~2000 problems and ~20k code solutions, \textsc{Llama 3.1 70B} is being fine-tuned for the first and second task. Time and space generation training sets sums up to ~22M tokens, prediction training sets to ~18-19M. Each complexity class of each problem includes 10 human examples, filtered following the same steps as the test set creation (see \cref{composition}). Models are fine-tuned for 10 epochs in instruct format. Results are displayed in \cref{table:finetuning_experiments}.

\section{Quantitative Analysis}
\label{quantitative_analysis}

\textbf{Understanding time-space complexity}~~~Looking at \cref{table:complexityeval}, all LLMs undergo a noticeable drop of performance on the combined task \textbf{Complexity Generation} compared to the individual tasks \textbf{Program Synthesis} and \textbf{Complexity Prediction}. Across all tasks, the top performing model remains \textsc{DeepSeek-R1 Llama 70B} with 64.2 and 29.2 $Pass@1$ on respectively time prediction and generation, except on space prediction where models tend to overthink and misunderstand the notion of extra space complexity, though explicitly described in the test prompts. Models tend to be even more misled when asked to \textit{"Optimize the solution while respecting the complexity requirement"}, which leads to average 12\% loss of performance for time generation $All@1$ in \cref{table:coefficient_ranking}, up to ~30\% for \textsc{GPT-4o} and \textsc{o1-mini}. 

