% \subsection{Debiased Inference} \label{sec:infer}
% In MER, class imbalance often introduces biases that negatively impact model performance. To mitigate these effects, we adopt a debiased inference technique inspired by~\cite{tan2024causally}, which adjusts the model's output logits without requiring additional training. Specifically, given an input tensor \( \textbf{x}^{OF}\in \mathbb{R}^{H\times W\times 3}\) and a zero input tensor \(\textbf{0}\in \mathbb{R}^{H\times W\times 3} \) with the same dimensions, the MERba model produces two logits tensors: \( \textbf{l}^{OF}\in \mathbb{R}^{C} \) and \( \textbf{l}^{zero}\in \mathbb{R}^{C} \), respectively. The debiased output logits \( \textbf{l}^{out}\in \mathbb{R}^{C} \) are then calculated as: $\textbf{l}^{out} = \textbf{l}^{OF} - \beta \cdot \textbf{l}^{zero}$, where $\beta$ denotes the debiasing rate. The final debiased prediction for the ME sample is obtained as: $\hat{y}=\arg\max(\textbf{l}^{out})$. This inference method effectively counteracts class imbalance, ensuring a more robust and fair prediction for MER tasks.

