\IEEEPARstart{R}{ecent} advancements in AI and machine learning have made it possible to perform highly complex computations and uncover intricate relationships that were once difficult to identify. These breakthroughs have paved the way for innovative methods capable of processing and organizing chaotic data sets more effectively. One such method is reservoir computing (RC).

Reservoir computing is a computational approach that harnesses the nonlinear interactions of nodes within a neural network-like structure, making it particularly well-suited for complex tasks like chaotic time series prediction and speech recognition. RC is built on a three-layer architecture consisting of an input layer, a reservoir layer, and an output layer.

The input layer connects real-world data to the network, feeding information into the reservoir layer. This reservoir layer comprises numerous interconnected nonlinear nodes that emulate the complex dynamics of a neural network. Process input using fixed random weights and internal feedback loops, generating transient responses. These responses are then processed in the output layer by calculating a linear weighted sum of the node states. RC is highly adaptable, capable of performing tasks such as input classification or specific non-linear transformations by adjusting node weights and modifying the reservoir's feedback structure.

In this experiment, a delay-based reservoir is implemented using a physical computing structure, forming a physical reservoir computing (PRC) system. Unlike traditional transistor-based architectures, PRC systems utilize the inherent features of physical materials to establish connections and recognize patterns within non-linear relationships. This approach enables faster and more cost-effective computation by leveraging the natural properties of the system without requiring additional components or complex calculations. The computational power of a PRC system is based on three key requirements: high dimensionality, nonlinearity, and fading memory. Using these features, we designed a delay-based PRC system as a molecular communications (MC) system in Smoldyn.

Molecular communication encodes information into molecules that are then received through ligand receptors on cell surfaces. Although MC systems face challenges such as intersymbol interference (ISI) from molecular diffusion and receptor positioning, as well as fading memory from the stochastic binding and unbinding of molecules, these seemingly problematic features can be advantageous in a PRC system. The nonlinearity and memory characteristics intrinsic to MC systems fulfill essential requirements for PRC, while ISI influences the system's memory capacity.

A PRC system utilizing molecular communications offers a wide range of applications in both medical and electronic fields. Using naturally occurring systems within the body to collect and process information, PRC systems can enable advanced biological anomaly detection, facilitating disease monitoring and treatment tracking through biomarkers.

In addition, PRC technology can be used to design biosensors that are more compatible with the natural functions of the body or environmental conditions, paving the way for complex hybrid structures that seamlessly integrate electronic and biological components. Furthermore, because of their adaptable nature, PRC-based sensors and devices can be more easily customized to specific computational tasks and datasets, offering greater reusability and flexibility compared to traditional sensors.

In the designed system, a cell covered with receptors processes time-delayed nonlinear data to predict future values, which are then compared with chaotic time series benchmarks like Mackey Glass and NARMA10. This research aims to demonstrate the computational capability of an end-to-end MC system, including ligand-receptor interactions, through the lens of reservoir computing.
