The spherical receiver is equipped with $N_R$ identical, monovalent ligand receptors. Each receptor can be either unbound or bound to one ligand, with reversible reaction:
\[
L + R
\overset{k_{\text{on}}}{\underset{k_{\text{off}}}{\longleftrightarrow}}
LR,
\]
where $L$ is the free ligand, $R$ is an unbound receptor, and $LR$ is a ligand-receptor complex, i.e., bound receptor. At the mean-field level, and assuming the local free ligand concentration is not significantly depleted by bound receptors, the time evolution of the \emph{mean} fraction of bound receptors, $b(t)=n_b(t)/N_R$, can be approximated by
\begin{equation}
    \frac{d\,b(t)}{d t}
    \;=\;
    k_{\mathrm{on}}\,c_T(t)\,\bigl[1 - b(t)\bigr]
    \;-\;
    k_{\mathrm{off}}\,b(t),
    \label{eq:mean_bound}
\end{equation}
where $k_{\mathrm{on}}$ and $k_{\mathrm{off}}$ are the forward (binding) and reverse (unbinding) rate constants, respectively, and $c_T(t)$ is the local ligand concentration at the receiver. 


Although the exact time-varying \emph{variance} of $b(t)$ under such nonstationary input is typically not available in closed form (due to correlated ligand arrivals and receptor occupancy fluctuations), we use particle-based spatial stochastic simulations (with Smoldyn (Fig. \ref{fig:concentration}) \cite{andrews2017smoldyn}) in Section~\ref{sec:results} to account for this stochasticity. For the present analytical treatment, \eqref{eq:mean_bound} provides a deterministic approximation to the \emph{mean} behavior of the ligand-receptor interactions.


