\subsection{Chaotic Time-Series Prediction}


We benchmark our MC reservoir computer on two widely used chaotic time‑series prediction tasks: \emph{Mackey--Glass} and \emph{NARMA10} \cite{wringe2025reservoir}. These tasks have become standards for assessing both algorithmic and physical RC architectures because they combine complex temporal dependencies, strong nonlinearities, and a requirement for long‑range memory. The goal is to learn a mapping from the present (and past) states of the time series to future values, using only a linear readout layer trained on the reservoir states.


\textbf{Performance Metric:} We quantify the predictive accuracy of the reservoir using the normalized root-mean-square error (NRMSE),
\[
\text{NRMSE} \;=\;\sqrt{\frac{\sum_{k=1}^{N_{\mathrm{test}}} [\,y_k - \hat{y}_k\,]^{2}}{\sum_{k=1}^{N_{\mathrm{test}}} [\,y_k - \bar{y}\,]^{2}}},
\]
where $y_k$ is the true target value at time $k$, $\hat{y}_k$ is the reservoir’s predicted value, $\bar{y}$ is the mean of all true target values, and $N_{\mathrm{test}}$ is the number of test samples. An NRMSE closer to $0$ indicates a more accurate prediction.

