\subsubsection{Post-Process Filtering}
Due to the inherent stochasticity of molecular diffusion and receptor-ligand interactions, especially in low-concentration regimes, the raw output of the molecular reservoir exhibits noise-driven fluctuations, as observed in Fig. \ref{fig:concentration}. These fluctuations, though biologically realistic, can obfuscate the underlying information-carrying trends. To address this, we applied a moving average filter to the received signal of receptor occupation $b(t)$, effectively attenuating high-frequency noise components while preserving meaningful temporal dynamics. The entire received signal sequence is filtered, the effects of which on the prediction task are shown in Fig. \ref{fig:predictionplot}. The post-processed received signals are observed to display outputs similar to those of the estimation layer to deterministic simulations, enabling more robust performance during inference. The change in error with respect to the length of the filtering window is presented in Fig. \ref{fig:movavgeffect}, the filter improves performance until a certain point, after which the loss of information becomes too great and leads to an increase in error. 

