\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

In this work, we have demonstrated, through cross‑validated deterministic and particle‑based stochastic simulations, that a canonical point‑to‑point MC channel naturally realizes a physical reservoir computer. By interpreting the time‑multiplexed receptor occupancy as virtual nodes, the system reliably forecasts Mackey--Glass and NARMA10 time series with low normalized error, despite substantial molecular noise. A comprehensive parameter sweep analysis revealed that computational power of the MC reservoir hinges on a delicate balance between several system parameters, including receptor binding kinetics, diffusion coefficients, and symbol intervals, all of which influence the memory and nonlinearity essential for effective reservoir computing. This analysis provides the initial guidelines for rationally optimizing MC systems for wetware AI. Future work will focus on extending the system to MC networks and multiple ligand-receptor pairs, and on implementing the proposed physical reservoir computer in experimental MC platforms.


\begin{comment}
In this work, we proposed a novel approach for employing molecular communication (MC) systems as physical reservoir computers. By treating the receptor occupation dynamics of a single spherical receiver as the nonlinear internal state and applying time-multiplexing to generate virtual nodes, we successfully implemented a delay-based reservoir computing framework using only the intrinsic biophysical properties of ligand-receptor interactions and diffusion. Both deterministic simulations in MATLAB and stochastic particle-based simulations via Smoldyn confirmed that the reservoir is capable of accurately performing chaotic time-series prediction tasks. Importantly, the predictive performance was shown to be sensitive to MC channel parameters, including binding rates, diffusion coefficients, and symbol intervals, all of which influence the memory and nonlinearity essential for effective reservoir computing. Post-processing via filtering further enhanced robustness under stochastic regimes.

Our findings demonstrate that even a minimal MC setup, traditionally considered only as a communication link, possesses sufficient dynamical richness to serve as a standalone computational substrate. This reconceptualization opens the door for integrating biochemical communication systems directly into intelligent nanoscale computing architectures. Future work may extend this framework to multi-receiver networks, heterogeneous ligand populations, or synthetic biological implementations, ultimately aiming toward physically realized wetware intelligence with embedded learning and adaptive capabilities.
\end{comment}

