\]
meaning:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Insertion Sort Threshold} = 3075
    \item \textbf{Parallel Merge Threshold} = 31291
    \item \textbf{Merge Algorithm Code} = 4 (\(\rightarrow\) LSD radix sort for large arrays)
    \item \textbf{NumPy Sort Threshold} = 99574
    \item \textbf{Tile Size} = 1418
\end{itemize}
These values instructs EvoSort when to switch to insertion sort for smaller subarrays (\(T_{\text{insertion}} = 3075\)), when to stop recursive splitting in mergesort (\(T_{\text{merge}} = 31291\)), and under which circumstances to invoke LSD radix sort (\(A_{\text{code}} = 4\)). The threshold for falling back on NumPy’s \verb|sort| is set at \(99574\), and the tile size of \(1418\) optimizes cache usage in merges and histogram-based routines \citep{Frigo1999}.

\vspace{1ex}
\paragraph*{Final Performance Comparison:}
Once the GA has identified these optimal parameters, EvoSort applies them to the full 10\,M-element array. The final runtime is measured at 0.2886\,s—slightly above the GA’s best reported time of 0.2416\,s due to minor sampling differences and overhead in the full run. Nevertheless, Figure~\ref{fig:ga_10M} (right) shows that EvoSort’s performance (0.2886\,s) significantly outperforms:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{NumPy Quicksort:} 0.8157\,s
    \item \textbf{NumPy Mergesort:} 0.9733\,s
\end{itemize}
In other words, EvoSort is roughly three to four times faster than NumPy’s default sorting routines for this dataset size. Moreover, the final validation confirms that the output of EvoSort exactly matches NumPy’s sorted array, ensuring correctness of the sorting process.

\vspace{1ex}
\paragraph*{Discussion:}
This example highlights the effectiveness of combining parallel sorting techniques with an on-the-fly GA parameter search. Even for a moderately large dataset (\(10^7\) elements), the GA converges quickly, often in fewer than 10 generations, to a configuration that delivers a substantial speedup over manually tuned or fixed-parameter methods. The process is fully automated: once the dataset size and random seed are specified, EvoSort’s GA driver runs in the background to discover these parameters, freeing users from time-consuming and error-prone manual optimization \citep{Deb2001,Deb2002}.

In summary, the 10\,M-element case exemplifies EvoSort’s ability to leverage the GA’s rapid convergence, identify the best algorithmic thresholds, and consistently outperform NumPy’s built-in sorting algorithms. This self-tuning paradigm underlines the broader potential of evolutionary metaheuristics in high-performance computing, especially for tasks where parameter configurations profoundly influence runtime.

