    \item \textbf{Diverse Beginnings:} The first generation showcased a broad spread in performance, with execution times varying dramatically. This variability set the stage for the GA to identify and amplify the most effective parameter combinations.
    \item \textbf{Rapid Convergence:} Over subsequent generations, the GA consistently refined the search space. By Generation~7, the average evaluation time stabilized at approximately 1.38 seconds, reflecting a robust convergence towards optimal settings \citep{Deb2001,Deb2002}.
    \item \textbf{Optimal Configuration:} The best individual found was \([2514,\, 24721,\, 4,\, 50840,\, 2020]\), which recorded an evaluation time of 1.3806 seconds. These parameters represent the thresholds for switching sorting methods and the specific tuning necessary to maximize performance.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{1ex}
\paragraph*{Final Performance Comparison:}
With the GA-optimized parameters in hand—and leveraging 256 threads for Numba’s parallel execution—EvoSort managed to sort the entire 1\,B-element array in just 0.9164 seconds. In stark contrast, NumPy’s quicksort and mergesort took 104.9122 seconds and 127.4918 seconds, respectively. The dramatic reduction in sorting time not only underscores the effectiveness of the GA-driven approach but also highlights EvoSort’s superior scalability.

\vspace{1ex}
\paragraph*{Discussion:}
This 1-billion-element benchmark powerfully illustrates how an adaptive, evolutionary approach can tame the complexity of massive datasets. The GA rapidly hones in on a high-performing configuration despite the challenging initial variability in execution times. The resulting performance—orders of magnitude faster than conventional NumPy sorting algorithms—demonstrates the practical benefits of automated parameter tuning in high-performance computing. 




