\section{Symbolic-Regression Performance Model}
\label{sec:symbolic}

Our GA-based auto-tuner reliably finds near-optimal sorting parameters, but incurs significant overhead (sampling and hundreds of fitness evaluations per run). To eliminate this cost, we applied symbolic regression to the GA outputs and derived closed-form formulas for each threshold as a function of the problem size \(n\). Additionally, during this process, we fixed the categorical choice to Block-Based LSD Radix Sort to enable a closed-form solution for the integer sorting parameters. This section presents those formulas, quantifies their accuracy, visualizes fit quality across four parameters, analyzes the mathematical properties of each fit, and finally shows end-to-end timing results when using the symbolic models in place of the GA loop.

\subsection{Quadratic Fit Formulas}
We model each threshold \(T(n)\) as a simple quadratic in
\[
  x = \log_{10} n,
  \quad
  T(n) = a\,x^2 + b\,x + c.
\]


The resulting formulas are:

\begin{align}
T_{\mathrm{ins}}(n)
&= \frac{18093685\,x^2}{726826}
  - \frac{227830214\,x}{693565}
  + \frac{1730747635}{502001},
  &\label{eq:Tinsertion}\\[6pt]
T_{\mathrm{par}}(n)
&= -\frac{4279813193\,x^2}{907161}
  + \frac{79199394278\,x}{983501}
  - \frac{309812890693}{956422},
  &\label{eq:Tparallel}\\[6pt]
T_{\mathrm{np}}(n)
&= -\frac{3680680444\,x^2}{890339}
  + \frac{39413203286\,x}{521933}
  - \frac{219719696809}{785367},
  &\label{eq:Tnumpy}\\[6pt]
T_{\mathrm{tile}}(n)
&= \frac{2451303315\,x^2}{877429}
  - \frac{7878849997\,x}{184645}
  + \frac{157328357967}{943252},
  &\label{eq:Ttile}
\end{align}

\subsection{Visual Fit Quality Across Parameters}
Figure~\ref{fig:all-trends} shows all four normalized GA-tuned thresholds (markers) and their quadratic symbolic fits (lines) on a logarithmic \(n\) axis. The close alignment across two orders of magnitude demonstrates that the quadratic models faithfully capture the GA output trend.

\begin{figure}[ht]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{comparison_of_parameter_trend.png}
  \caption{Normalized GA-tuned thresholds and symbolic-regression fits versus dataset size \(n\).}
  \label{fig:all-trends}
\end{figure}

For deeper inspection, Figures~\ref{fig:tile-trend}--\ref{fig:ins-trend} plot each parameter’s raw GA values against \(n\) alongside the fitted curve. Despite the wide distribution of data, it turns out the parameters are not that sensitive to produce good performance of our algorithm.

\begin{figure}[ht]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{tile_size_vs-dataset_size.png}
  \caption{Tile size: GA picks (red dots) and quadratic fit (blue line). The model captures a shallow minimum near \(4\times10^7\) elements.}
  \label{fig:tile-trend}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[ht]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{numpy_sort_threshold_vs_dataset_size.png}
  \caption{NumPy-sort threshold: GA results (red) and symbolic fit (blue). Peak hand-off at approximately \(1.4\times10^9\) elements.}
  \label{fig:np-trend}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[ht]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{parallel_merge_threshold_vs_dataset_size.png}
  \caption{Parallel-merge threshold: GA picks (red) and fit (blue). Optimal block size peaks near \(3.5\times10^8\) elements, then declines.}
  \label{fig:par-trend}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[ht]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{insertion_sort_threshold_vs_dataset_size.png}
  \caption{Insertion-sort threshold: GA data (red) and quadratic fit (blue). Convex-up trend with minimum at approximately \(4\times10^6\) elements.}
  \label{fig:ins-trend}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Residual Analysis and Model Parsimony}
We examined residuals \(r_i = T_{\mathrm{GA}}(n_i) - T_{\mathrm{pred}}(n_i)\) across the training set:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Insertion cutoff}: residuals within \(\pm2000\) elements, no visible bias across \(n\).
  \item \textbf{Parallel-merge}: residuals under \(\pm10000\) in the mid-range, slightly larger at extremes, justifying the concave-down model.
  \item \textbf{NumPy hand-off}: symmetric residual pattern around the peak near \(10^9\).
  \item \textbf{Tile size}: minimal residual at the quadratic vertex near \(4.3\times10^7\), increasing smoothly for smaller or larger \(n\).
\end{itemize}
Limiting model complexity to degree 2 prevents overfitting.

\subsection{Analytical Properties of the Quadratics}
Each fit \(T(n)=a\,x^2 + b\,x + c\) with \(x=\log_{10}n\) has curvature \(a\), extremum \(x^* = -b/(2a)\), and characteristic asymptotic behavior:
\begin{description}
  \item[\(T_{\mathrm{ins}}\)] coefficient \(a > 0\) (convex), minimum at \(x^*\approx6.60\) (\(n\approx4\times10^6\)). For large \(n\), \(T_{\mathrm{ins}}\) grows approximately as \(a(\log_{10}n)^2\).
  \item[\(T_{\mathrm{par}}\)] coefficient \(a < 0\) (concave), maximum at \(x^*\approx8.54\) (\(n\approx3.5\times10^8\)), reflecting diminishing returns of larger merge blocks.
  \item[\(T_{\mathrm{np}}\)] coefficient \(a < 0\), maximum at \(x^*\approx9.14\) (\(n\approx1.4\times10^9\)), matching the ideal hand-off point to NumPy’s sort.
  \item[\(T_{\mathrm{tile}}\)] coefficient \(a > 0\) (convex), minimum at \(x^*\approx7.63\) (\(n\approx4.3\times10^7\)); tile sizes increase for very small or very large \(n\) to balance parallel efficiency and overhead.
\end{description}

\subsection{End-to-End Timing Comparison}
Finally, we replaced the GA loop with these symbolic equations and benchmarked EvoSort’s wall-clock time against NumPy’s \texttt{sort()} on four array sizes. Figure~\ref{fig:timing-comparison} and Table~\ref{tab:results} demonstrate that the symbolic-parameter variant sorts 5 billion elements in under 4 seconds, over 165x faster than NumPy, while requiring zero tuning overhead.

