\section*{Data Availability Statement}

In our experiments, the large arrays are generated using NumPy's \texttt{np.random.randint} function. To ensure that the data distribution is exactly reproducible, we set a fixed random seed using \texttt{np.random.seed(seed)} before generating the arrays. For any given array of size \( n \), each element \( x_i \) is drawn from a uniform distribution over the interval \([-10^9, +10^9]\). Mathematically, this is represented as:
\[
x_i \sim \mathcal{U}(-10^9, 10^9) \quad \text{for } i = 1, 2, \dots, n.
\]
Using a fixed seed guarantees that the same sequence of random numbers is produced on every run, which makes our experiments fully reproducible.

All generated datasets, as well as the corresponding sorting outputs and performance metrics, are produced at runtime. 


\section*{Code Availability Statement}

EvoSort is implemented as a modular Python package. The codebase includes all core sorting algorithms as well as the symbolic quadratic models described in this paper. The full source code of the package is available from the authors upon reasonable request.


