\section{A mathematical explanation of Dynamic Topic Modelling}
\label{sec:appendix:mathematical_explanation_of_DTMs}

As we discuss in the main text (see Sec.~\ref{subsec:topic_modelling_using_dtms}), we use \textit{Dynamic Topic Models} (DTMs) to detect the relevant topics in the corpus of Ideas y Valores \citep{BleiLDA,BleiDTM}. This section dives deeper into the mathematics behind this model; a less mathematical explanation (which should be more than enough for the purposes of this paper) can be found in the aforementioned section.

This appendix assumes familiarity with probability theory and statistical inference. In particular, we expect the reader to know about distributions like the Categorical (or Multinomial) and Dirichlet, and about how to infer the parameters of a probability distribution from data using variational inference.

\subsection{Latent Dirichlet Allocation}

Understanding DTMs requires understanding Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA) models first. LDA is a type of probabilistic generative model, meaning that it describes a way in which the data could be generated according to probability distributions.

LDA formalizes the process by which documents are generated as follows: A corpus $\mathcal{C} = \{\bm{d}_1, \dots, \bm{d}_M\}$ is made of a collection of documents $\bm{d}_m = (w_1, \dots, w_N)$, which are in turn made of words $w_j$. Broadly speaking, LDA assumes that authors generate documents by
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Deciding the document's length, or number of words $N$.
    \item Deciding the proportions of each topic to use. Say we have $K$ topics in total, then this would correspond to specifying a vector $\bm{\theta} = (\theta_1, \dots, \theta_K)$ such that all entries add up to $1.0$. In other words, $\theta_k$ corresponds to the proportion (or percentage) of topic $k$ in the document. Each topic $\bm{\beta}_1,\dots, \bm{\beta}_K$ is a vector of percentages over all words.
    \item For each of the $N$ words, the author first decides which topic it belongs to according to the proportions in $\bm{\theta}$. Say topic $k$ was selected. The author then decides on which word to choose next according to the vector of proportions $\bm{\beta}_k = (\beta_{k1}, \dots, \beta_{kV})$, where $V$ is the total number of unique words in all documents.
\end{enumerate}

As a minimal example, say we have two topics $\bm{\theta} = (\theta_1, \theta_2) = (0.7, 0.3)$, and a small vocabulary of three words ``a'', ``b'', and ``c''. The topic vectors could for example be $\bm{\beta}_1 = (0.1, 0.0, 0.9)$ and $\bm{\beta}_2 = (0.0, 1.0, 0.0)$.  According to an LDA, each word an author wants to write is chosen by first looking at the proportions on $\bm{\theta}$ (thus selecting the first topic 70\% of the time, and the second topic 30\% of the time), and then looking at the distributions inside $\bm{\beta}_k$ where $k$ is either 1 or 2 depending on the aforementioned choice.

The following is a simple script that uses Python and \texttt{numpy} to simulate this generative process for a 5-word document.

\begin{lstfloat}
\begin{lstlisting}
import numpy as np

words = ["a", "b", "c"]

# theta
topic_proportions = [0.7, 0.3]

#  beta_1
topic_1_proportions = [0.2, 0.0, 0.8]  

#  beta_2
topic_2_proportions = [0.0, 1.0, 0.0]

for _ in range(5):
    topic_id = np.random.choice(
        [1, 2],
        p=topic_proportions
    )

    if topic_id == 1:
        word = np.random.choice(
            words,
            p=topic_1_proportions
        )
    else:
        word = np.random.choice(
            words,
            p=topic_2_proportions
        )

    print(word)

\end{lstlisting}
\caption{Generating a document given a set of topic proportions. An LDA materializes the \texttt{topic\_proportions} using samples from a Dirichlet distribution, learning its parameters, \texttt{topic\_1\_proportions} and \texttt{topic\_2\_proportions} from a corpus of documents.}
\label{code:generative-model_LDA}
\end{lstfloat}

Using a simple script to simulate this generative process (see Code~\ref{code:generative-model_LDA}), a 5-word document looks like this: ``c - b - a - c - c''. Notice how most of the times the letter ``c'' was selected, since it is the most likely word of the most likely topic for this specific configuration. The letter ``b'' (which is only possible in topic 2) is only selected once, as is the letter ``a''.

\textbf{How do we choose the parameters $\bm{\theta}$ and $\bm{\beta}_k$ to faithfully represent a corpus that is given to us?} This question is answered through statistical inference, which is a field of statistics in which distribution parameters are identified automatically through data.

One way to model the selection of topic proportions is to use the Dirichlet distribution, since its outputs are precisely vectors of proportions. Recall that the Dirichlet distribution is parameterized by a collection of positive real values $\bm{\alpha} = (\alpha_1, \dots,\alpha_k)$, such that samples from the distribution $\bm{\theta}\sim \text{Dirichlet}(\bm{\alpha})$ are \textbf{probability vectors}, defined as list of elements that are between 0 and 1, and which all add up to 1. Each element $\theta_i$ of a sample from a Dirichlet contains, then, a proportion of the entire probability, which is determined by how its respective $\alpha_i$ stands in proportion to $\sum_i \alpha_i$. As an example, a minimal Python script in which samples from a Dirichlet are taken can be found in Code~\ref{code:sampling-from-Dirichlet}.

\begin{lstfloat}
\begin{lstlisting}
import numpy as np

alpha = [2.0, 2.0, 1.0]
samples = np.random.dirichlet(alpha, 5)
print(samples)
\end{lstlisting}
\caption{Sampling from a Dirichlet distribution using NumPy.}
\label{code:sampling-from-Dirichlet}
\end{lstfloat}

\begin{figure*}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{figures/fig7_dirichletsamples.pdf}
    \caption{Samples from a Dirichlet distribution with $\bm{\alpha} = (2.0, 2.0, 1.0)$. Notice how the third class is, in proportion, generally smaller than the first two. Expected values for the categories can be computed easily from the vector $\bm{\alpha}$: the average value of a category is given by its respective $\alpha_i$ divided by the sum of all $\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_K$. In this particular example, if we were to keep sampling, the average value of e.g. the first category would be $2.0 / (2.0 + 2.0 + 1.0) = 2/5$. The variance between these samples is also encoded in our choice for $\bm{\alpha}$.}
    \label{fig:appendix:dirichlet_samples}
\end{figure*}

These five samples are shown as barplots in Fig.~\ref{fig:appendix:dirichlet_samples}. $\bm{\alpha}$ governs the distribution by telling what the average value of each $\theta_i$ should be, as well as its variance. If we sample from this distribution with $\bm{\alpha} = (2.0, 2.0, 1.0)$ several times, then the average of the first element will tend towards $2/5$ (i.e. $\alpha_1$ divided by the sum of all values in $\bm{\alpha}$).

With this, we can re-write the generative process using the language of distributions: An author writes an article by
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Deciding on the document's length $N$.
    \item Sampling topic proportions $\bm{\theta} = (\theta_1, \dots, \theta_K)$ from a $\text{Dirichlet}(\bm{\alpha})$ where $\bm{\alpha} = (\alpha_1, \dots, \alpha_K)$.
    \item For word $w_n$, the author decides on a topic by choosing on an index $k$ according to the probabilities in $\bm{\theta}$, and then choosing on a word from the vocabulary according to the probabilities in $\bm{\beta}_k$.\footnote{In probabilistic terms, we would say $k\sim \text{Categorical}(\bm{\theta})$, and $w_n\sim\text{Categorical}(\bm{\beta}_k)$.}
\end{enumerate}

In LDA, the Dirichlet parameter $\bm{\alpha}$ and the topic proportions $\bm{\beta}_1,\dots,\bm{\beta}_K$ are estimated from all documents using \textbf{statistical inference}. These parameters determine the structure of topics in a corpus, and they can be inferred from the documents alone. In a nutshell, statistical inference determine the parameters of a given probability distribution or generative model in an optimization process: we start with an initial guess for what $\bm{\alpha}$ and the topic proportions could be, and we tweak them iteratively to make the documents in the dataset more likely.

Circling back to the original example in Code~\ref{code:generative-model_LDA}, if we start with a collection of documents, the goal of this optimization process is akin to recreating said documents by modifying the parameters in \texttt{topic\_proportions}, \texttt{topic\_1\_proportions} and \texttt{topic\_2\_proportions}.

The specifics of the actual optimization procedure used by \citep{BleiLDA} are beyond the scope of this explanation. An interested reader could explore the article where LDAs were proposed, or recent surveys on the subjects (namely variational inference) \citep{bleisurvey}.

To summarize, LDA is a method for identifying topics in a corpus of documents without previous annotation. It assumes that documents are made by (i) selecting topic proportions (e.g. 70\% Kantian ethics, 20\% philosophy of mind...) by sampling from a Dirichlet distribution with parameters $\bm{\alpha}=(\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_K)$, (ii) selecting a topic $\bm{\beta}_k$ for each word, and then sampling from the vocabulary with the probabilities specified by said topic i.e. $\bm{\beta}_k = (\beta_{k1},\dots, \beta_{kV}$). The topic proportions and the distributions of words for each topic are learned by maximizing the likelihood of the data.

\subsection{Dynamic Topic Modelling}

The previous section introduced Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA), a method for unsupervised learning of topic distributions inside documents.
In this algorithm, the topic proportions $\bm{\theta}$ and the probabilities of words in a given topic $\bm{\beta}_k$ are learned for the entire corpus, without regard to the year a given document was published. It would be more natural to assume that the structure of topics in a given corpus evolves over time, and that the words used to describe a topic in one decade may not match another.

To incorporate time into LDAs, Blei and Lafferty \citep{BleiDTM} consider a variation of their original model in which the variables that encode topic proportions and word probabilities \textit{evolve over time}. The variables that encode the topic proportions and distributions $\bm{\alpha}_t$ and $\bm{\beta}_{k,t}$ are only slight variations of the previous time slice $\bm{\alpha}_{t-1}$ and $\bm{\beta}_{t-1}$. 

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{figures/fig8_timeevolution.pdf}
    \caption{How topic proportions evolve over time slices. At each time step, the new topic proportions are slight modifications of the previous ones, defined by sampling from a Gaussian distribution in a high-dimensional space. This can be expressed mathematically with the relationship $\bm{\alpha}_{t} \,|\, \bm{\alpha}_{t-1} \sim \mathcal{N}(\bm{\alpha}_{t-1}, \delta^2I)$.}
    \label{fig:appendix:topic-proportion-evo}
\end{figure}

Such a model of evolution can be represented by sampling from a Gaussian distribution. Fig.~\ref{fig:appendix:topic-proportion-evo} shows this idea for two topics. Sampling $\alpha_{t}$ from $\mathcal{N}(\alpha_{t-1}, \sigma^2 I)$ corresponds to taking a random step around the previous topic proportions, and the same can be said for sampling $\bm{\beta}_{k, t}$ from $\mathcal{N}(\bm{\beta}_{k, t-1}, \sigma^2I)$.\footnote{Another way to understand DTMs is as a collection of several LDAs (one per time slice), linked by the fact that the topic proportions and distributions of words in a topic need to be slight variations from one time slice to the next.}

To transform these real numbers $\bm{\alpha}_t$ into probability vectors, LDA relied on considering them as parameters of a Dirichlet distribution. In DTMs, the authors propose to use the Softmax transformation (which takes as input vectors of real numbers, and outputs a probability vector):
$$\bm{\alpha}_t = (\alpha_{1, t}, \dots, \alpha_{K, t}) \mapsto \text{Softmax}(\bm{\alpha}_t) = \left(\frac{\exp(\alpha_{k, t})}{\sum_{k'=1}^K \exp(\alpha_{k', t})}\right)$$

Let's describe DTMs as a generative model, following our notation of LDAs and the algorithm presented in the original article \citep{BleiDTM}: At a given time slice $t$, the author
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Evolves all topics from the previous time slice: $\bm{\beta}_{k, t} \,|\, \bm{\beta}_{k, t-1}  \sim \mathcal{N}(\bm{\beta}_{k, t-1}, \sigma^2 I)$.
    \item Evolves the topic proportions from the previous time slice: $\bm{\alpha}_{t} \,|\, \bm{\alpha}_{t-1} \sim \mathcal{N}(\bm{\alpha}_{t-1}, \delta^2I)$.
    \item For each document:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item Chooses the topic proportions for the document by drawing $\eta \sim \mathcal{N}(\alpha_t, a^2 I)$ and mapping it to a probability vector using $\text{Softmax}$.
        \item For each word:
        \begin{enumerate}
            \item Decides on a topic $k$ according to the proportions on $\text{Softmax}(\eta)$.
            \item Chooses a word $w$ from topic $k$ by sampling from $\text{Softmax}(\bm{\beta}_{t, k})$.
        \end{enumerate}
    \end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}

The parameters $\bm{\alpha}_t$, $\bm{\beta}_{t, k}$ are also learned using statistical inference. The fact that we are evolving the topic proportions from one time slice to another using the Gaussian distribution allows us to compute the mean and variance 
of $\bm{\alpha}_t$ given the past and $\bm{\beta}_t$ in closed form.

